============================================================
WORK MODE RULES
============================================================

Use one mode only:
- PLAN: audit, explain, sequence, decide scope
- ACT: edit code, build, verify minimal result

Do not mix PLAN and ACT.

For PLAN:
- Do not edit files.
- Do not create files.
- Do not move files.
- Do not delete files.
- Do not run build unless explicitly requested.
- Do not mutate database.
- Do not run broad UAT.
- Read only the allowed files.
- Stop after producing audit/plan report.

For ACT:
- Work on one vertical slice only.
- Do not open a broad scope.
- Do not rewrite the whole backend/frontend.
- Do not touch unrelated modules.
- Do not run long UAT unless the user explicitly asks for final UAT.
- Do not create test data unless the current task explicitly requires it.
- Do not mutate database for exploratory testing.
- Prefer code patch + build first.
- Mark manual/API tests as PENDING for final verification when appropriate.

Token and time economy:
- Do not repeat UAT that already passed unless the touched code affects that flow.
- Do not login repeatedly unless needed.
- Do not run broad endpoint sweeps after every small patch.
- For code batches: audit files → patch → build → short report.
- Large API/manual UAT should be reserved for final verification gates.

============================================================
KOST48 V5 ARCHITECTURE DIRECTION
============================================================

Current architecture direction:
- KOST48 V5 — Multi-App Shared-DB Architecture.
- Not a total rewrite.
- Not pure microservices.
- Not separate database yet.
- Shared PostgreSQL remains.
- PrismaService remains shared.
- Migration style: greenfield shell + brownfield logic extraction.
- No distributed transaction.
- No service-to-service HTTP in Phase 0/1 unless explicitly decided.
- Existing business logic must be respected and extracted gradually.
- ChatGPT is the command center.
- Cline is a local repo auditor or bounded coding agent only.

Target backend apps:

1. core-api
   - owns Stay lifecycle writes
   - owns Room occupancy/status writes
   - owns manual check-in
   - owns booking approval
   - owns checkout final
   - owns renew execution
   - owns meter promotion
   - owns lifecycle-mutating payment approval until command boundary is designed

2. tenant-api
   - tenant booking create/my
   - payment submission create/my
   - checkout request create/view
   - renew request create/view
   - tenant invoice/stay read-only
   - notifications read
   - must not execute lifecycle finalization
   - must not mutate Stay lifecycle
   - must not mutate Room occupancy/status

3. staff-api
   - tickets
   - room view
   - inventory read-only
   - maintenance/task future
   - should be early low-risk extraction candidate

4. finance-api
   - invoices read/list/detail
   - invoice payments read
   - payment review queue read
   - finance reports
   - expenses/deposit/damage later
   - payment approval that mutates Stay/Room/Meter/Deposit remains core-api first

5. marketing-api
   - public rooms
   - public room detail
   - gallery/public profile/landing later
   - read-only first
   - should be early extraction candidate

6. owner-api
   - deferred
   - do not create early
   - avoid creating a second mini-monolith
   
============================================================
V5 HIGH-RISK FLOWS — DO NOT MOVE / DO NOT PATCH CASUALLY
============================================================

Do not move, extract, rewrite, or patch these unless the user explicitly asks and a PLAN audit has been approved:

- PaymentSubmissionsService.approveSubmission()
- StaysService.create()
- StaysService.complete()
- StaysService.renewStay()
- TenantBookingsService.approveBooking()
- Checkout final
- Renew admin approval/execution
- Room status writes
- Meter promotion
- Deposit settlement
- Damage/penalty schema

Ownership:
- These stay in core-api first.
- finance-api may read/review payment queue later, but approval that mutates lifecycle remains core-api.
- tenant-api may create/view requests/submissions, but must not execute lifecycle finalization.
- checkout request tenant create/view may be tenant-api later; admin approve/reject/final checkout remains core-api.
- renew request tenant create/view may be tenant-api later; admin approve/reject/execution remains core-api.
- payment submission tenant create/my may be tenant-api later; approval remains core-api until audited command boundary exists.

